# Rules for the UI Kit Project

## File Structure

- Components must be in the `lib/` directory
- Each component must have its own folder and include:
  - The component file
  - A story file
  - A unit test file

## Naming Conventions

- Components should use PascalCase (e.g., Button.tsx)
- Story files should follow the pattern ComponentName.stories.tsx
- Interfaces should use PascalCase (e.g., ButtonProps)
- Variables and functions should use camelCase

## React Components

- Components must be implemented using native HTML elements as the base (e.g., <button>, <input>, <a>, etc.).
- Extend the original attributes of the corresponding HTML element using React interfaces (e.g., React.ButtonHTMLAttributes for buttons).
- The props interface must include all variants and behaviors detected in the Figma design.
- Keep components functional and small
- Define TypeScript types for all props
- Document important props using JSDoc
- For more complex components, create partials in a `partials/` sub-directory

## Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
- Keep Tailwind classes organized and reusable
- Use tailwind-variants (tv) to manage component states and variants
- Define custom variants that complement React Aria functionality
- Use utilities like focusRing for a consistent user experience
- Provide default variant values using defaultVariants in tv()
- Avoid inline styles

## Testing

- Write unit tests for components
- Test files should follow the pattern ComponentName.spec.tsx
- Use Vitest for testing
- Maintain good test coverage

## Documentation

- Document important props with JSDoc
- Keep README.md up to date
- Provide usage examples in Storybook stories
- Stories and Documentation are in English

## Git

- Make atomic commits
- Use descriptive commit messages
- Keep .gitignore updated

## TypeScript

- Use strict types
- Avoid `any`
- Define interfaces for all props
- Use type inference when possible

## Accessibility

The design system must offer accessible components. We follow a native-first philosophy using semantic HTML and progressive enhancement where needed.

| Requirement             | Description                                                                   |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Native elements         | Always prefer `<button>`, `<a>`, `<input>`, etc. for semantic correctness.    |
| Keyboard navigation     | All components must be fully operable via keyboard (`Tab`, `Enter`, `Space`). |
| Focus management        | Manage focus explicitly on modals, drawers, and toasts when needed.           |
| ARIA attributes         | Use only when necessary to expose dynamic states or relationships.            |
| Screen reader support   | Ensure components are understandable with screen readers.                     |
| Color contrast          | Follow WCAG guidelines for contrast ratios.                                   |
| Focus indicators        | Show visible focus using consistent Tailwind focus utilities.                 |
| Error messages          | Must be programmatically associated and announced when needed.                |
| Labels and descriptions | Use `<label>`, `aria-labelledby`, and `aria-describedby` properly.            |
| Alternative text        | Provide `alt` for images and `aria-label` for icon-only buttons.              |
| Accessible roles        | Use roles (`dialog`, `menu`, etc.) only when enhancing behavior.              |
| No flashing content     | Avoid flashing or blinking content.                                           |

